{:edb/db-before-t 1352 :edb/db-after-t 1353 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534665 50 #inst "2026-10-08T03:39:26.212Z" 13194139534665 true] [17592186092061 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/a43a1658a16151c0306702959c50e8d0288077983ad50a084b39703e206469c0.png)\n\nConsider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ shown above. Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534665 true] [17592186092061 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/a43a1658a16151c0306702959c50e8d0288077983ad50a084b39703e206469c0.png)\n\nConsider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ shown above. Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534665 false] [17592186092061 1145 "Consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $A,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/414cb72ff004fe4c5898a0e2c7b60b4b3651473e776388fad21efd8fc9ae6165.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $A\\mathbf{v},$ we need to rotate $\\mathbf{v}$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/b24d8d460b7833a49f2bd7183f0f4b5d22d94a7d3b1d3bb9eb51beff9eeb7ef6.png)" 13194139534665 true] [17592186092061 1145 "Consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $A,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/414cb72ff004fe4c5898a0e2c7b60b4b3651473e776388fad21efd8fc9ae6165.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $A\\mathbf{v},$ we need to rotate $\\mathbf{v}$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/b24d8d460b7833a49f2bd7183f0f4b5d22d94a7d3b1d3bb9eb51beff9eeb7ef6.png)" 13194139534665 false] [17592186092170 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302239 13194139534665 true] [17592186092170 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302260 13194139534665 true] [17592186092171 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302246 13194139534665 true] [17592186092171 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302253 13194139534665 true] [17592186092219 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302267 13194139534665 true] [17592186092465 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534665 true] [17592186092465 1100 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,-2), (-1, 2), (1, 1)$. Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S^{′}$, the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation $R_{- π}$ by an angle of $π$ radians about the origin?\n\n1. $(1, 2)$\n2. $(1,-2)$\n3. $(-1,-1)$" 13194139534665 false] [17592186092465 1100 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,-2),(-1,2),(1,1).$  Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(1,2)$\n- $(1,-2)$\n- $(-1,-1)$" 13194139534665 true] [17592186092465 1145 "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by the same angle. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi },$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,2),(1,-2),(-1,-1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\"" 13194139534665 true] [17592186092773 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534665 true] [17592186092773 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/d111b1df75cfaa0d86d17c06584c57b19ca4711ae3e6720e68d1704fd72d86ae.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534665 true] [17592186092773 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/931/Source/Images/q-81450.png)\n\nConsi