gin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/f154267ed0a528b1e9fbec40064b090560410fe76dab00070e13fb3d1978b59c.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/8ea257c65769c4d6f67f55ce6533c1bfdfe45debcf1ba532b9ec3f717238507a.png)" 13194139534665 false] [17592186302239 1017 #edb/ref 17592186302240 13194139534665 true] [17592186302239 1089 "q-11961" 13194139534665 true] [17592186302239 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534665 true] [17592186302239 1100 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,-3),(0,5),(2,-5).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(3,-2)$\n- $(-5,0)$\n- $(5,2)$" 13194139534665 true] [17592186302239 1113 #uuid "1bc23a19-df35-5757-b9a8-0b3bf67f1e4c" 13194139534665 true] [17592186302239 1145 "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{\\pi /2},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(3,-2),(-5,0),(5,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139534665 true] [17592186302240 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302241 13194139534665 true] [17592186302240 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302242 13194139534665 true] [17592186302240 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302243 13194139534665 true] [17592186302240 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302244 13194139534665 true] [17592186302240 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302245 13194139534665 true] [17592186302240 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534665 true] [17592186302240 1041 "selection" 13194139534665 true] [17592186302240 1104 #edb/ref 17592186302241 13194139534665 true] [17592186302240 1110 #uuid "efd2d3ff-916a-554f-b5ff-99fc73d2ad5d" 13194139534665 true] [17592186302241 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139534665 true] [17592186302241 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534665 true] [17592186302241 1111 #uuid "fbc60a6d-2862-52aa-b8d3-7f5efd1a1738" 13194139534665 true] [17592186302242 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534665 true] [17592186302242 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534665 true] [17592186302242 1111 #uuid "1c46cf2d-9bff-5f3b-8ecf-f5bd8754777f" 13194139534665 true] [17592186302243 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534665 true] [17592186302243 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534665 true] [17592186302243 1111 #uuid "9506c4ca-6a11-5c3d-b959-ee562bbba4e3" 13194139534665 true] [17592186302244 1034 "III only" 13194139534665 true] [17592186302244 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534665 true] [17592186302244 1111 #uuid "33c35414-c1a7-5d57-9d69-e73a67690ce3" 13194139534665 true] [17592186302245 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534665 true] [17592186302245 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534665 true] [17592186302245 1111 #uuid "52109998-cd20-53b1-a3e0-e455f318a62f" 13194139534665 true] [17592186302246 1017 #edb/ref 17592186302247 13194139534665 true] [17592186302246 1089 "q-160374" 13194139534665 true] [17592186302246 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534665 true] [17592186302246 1100 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(4,-3),(2,-2),(1,3).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(-4,3)$\n- $(-2,2)$\n- $(-1,-3)$" 13194139534665 true] [17592186302246 1113 #uuid "9ec38e30-b9a4-5c3b-89e7-0bc6ddab674a" 13194139534665 true] [17592186302246 1145 "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by the same angle. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi },$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-4,3),(-2,2),(-1,-3).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139534665 true] [17592186302247 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302248 13194139534665 true] [17592186302247 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302249 13194139534665 true] [17592186302247 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302250 13194139534665 true] [17592186302247 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302251 13194139534665 true] [17592186302247 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302252 13194139534665 true] [17592186302247 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534665 t