ad5985bb0a8155857dca709996.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534665 true] [17592186302267 1113 #uuid "02463a6a-1144-577b-b2ee-cd0371f579ff" 13194139534665 true] [17592186302267 1145 "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/4e0e5c9cee037bded1f5739af35c3a8acfaa4d4e6f469b3e50afe69b96990e39.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/358dcedefd029cdbd07f3c64b1264e568bfbac134b92a607e9d55c632c277b13.png)" 13194139534665 true] [17592186302268 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302269 13194139534665 true] [17592186302268 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302270 13194139534665 true] [17592186302268 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302271 13194139534665 true] [17592186302268 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302272 13194139534665 true] [17592186302268 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302273 13194139534665 true] [17592186302268 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534665 true] [17592186302268 1041 "selection" 13194139534665 true] [17592186302268 1104 #